{"reference":"Job 32","book":"Job","chapter":32,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Elihu in the larger story","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Elihu enters after Job and the three friends have stopped speaking, angry with both Job and the friends. He says that his youth made him wait before speaking, then delivers four uninterrupted speeches before the divine answer. His role remains debated: some readers see him as preparing for that answer, while others see him as another inadequate speaker. His absence from the opening list of characters, the divine address, and the final rebuke does not settle the question.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_032_423bfe98a99071c74b88d5aa"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Job’s endurance","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Elsewhere, Job is named as an example connected with suffering, patience, endurance, and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. In that larger sense, Job’s steadfastness can describe persistence through the story and its outcome; it should not be taken to require silent endurance of abuse or to forbid seeking safety and protection.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_032_be2f8758305f00ae06e9ba3c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}